Subject: [PATCH 1/7] radv/ps5: an emulated indirect task draw costs what its count buffer draws

An emulated indirect task draw (vkCmdDrawMeshTasksIndirect[Count]EXT with a
task shader, radv_task_emulated) recorded, for every one of its maxDrawCount
draws, every chunk maxTaskWorkGroupTotalCount could need: 64 chunks a draw at
a 256-byte payload, 256 at 3,200 bytes, each a task dispatch, a records
prepass of the rings' entries and a draw, whatever the count buffer held.
On the console that is about 148 us (256 bytes) and 311 us (3,200 bytes) of
GPU time and 26-104 us of CPU time per draw slot: count@64 took 9.2 ms and
count@4096 586 ms with a count of 1, and the stream grew by about 16,000
words a draw slot.

The draws now go in groups of up to 4,096, and a group's chunks take its
draws' task workgroups one draw after the other, so that a chunk holds as
many draws as fit:

- A prepass (radv_meta_task_table, one workgroup of 512) reads the count
  buffer and the group's draws, scans their workgroup counts in LDS and
  writes the group's table (each draw's grid and first workgroup among the
  group's) and each chunk's task dispatch, records prepass dispatch and
  draw count, 0 past the group's workgroups.
- A chunk's task shader finds its workgroup's draw in the table, a uniform
  binary search of at most 12 scalar loads (radv_nir_lower_abi.c), takes
  its grid, workgroup ID and draw ID from it, and writes the draw's index
  to its mesh draw record, where the mesh shaders read their draw ID. Its
  ring entry is unchanged: its index in the chunk.
- The records prepass is dispatched from the chunk's count (a workgroup of
  32 records, or one workgroup with a publish ring) instead of one
  invocation per ring entry, and leaves the records' draw IDs alone.

As many chunks are recorded as the group's own maximum needs, up to a
budget of task workgroups for a group (RADV_PS5_TASK_WORKGROUPS, by default
maxTaskWorkGroupTotalCount, so that any one draw is whole) and 1,024 for a
draw command; workgroups past them are not drawn, which the first such
command tells on stderr. A direct task draw is unchanged.

The stream is no longer split while such a draw is recorded: its chunks
emit their draws without the draw's state, which a split would have reset
to the preamble's (a large count could do so before).

(cherry picked from commit da67d3ea2876373b13620f3723919c4238eeccb9)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

